{{nihongo|'''Isao Tomita'''|富田 功|Tomita Isao|born April 14, 1934}} is a Sanese politician and lawyer who served as the [[Prime Minister of Okaiken|45th Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from February 1993 to August 1995, leading the first [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] government following the party's two-year exile in opposition. Tomita's premiership marked the beginning of the LDP's "Long Restoration"—a twenty-three-year period of continuous conservative rule that lasted until 2016. His administration is primarily remembered for its ambitious but ultimately unsuccessful attempt to reverse the [[Lost Decade|deflationary spiral]] following the collapse of the [[Go-go 8090|bubble economy]], and for re-establishing the LDP's political dominance through a pragmatic three-party coalition with the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] and the [[New Tohoku Democratic Party|NTD]].

A veteran backroom negotiator from [[Akita Prefecture]] in the rural north, Tomita was widely regarded as a competent but uninspiring figure—a "maintenance man" rather than a visionary. His rise to the premiership was less a product of personal ambition than of the LDP's desperate need for a stabilizing figure who could reunite its fractured factions after the humiliation of the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]] and the chaotic revolving-door premiership of 1991. His tenure demonstrated both the strengths and the limits of coalition governance in a nation still reeling from the implosion of its economic miracle.

==Early life and education==

Isao Tomita was born on April 14, 1934, in the city of [[Yurihonjō]], [[Akita Prefecture]], in the snow country of northern Okaiken. His father, {{nihongo|Tomita Gihei|富田 義平}}, was a rice broker and local assemblyman who served three terms on the Yurihonjō City Council. His mother, {{nihongo|Tomita Chiyo|富田 千代}}, was the daughter of a [[Shoto]] priest at the local Kumano Shrine. The family was respected but not wealthy; Akita in the 1930s was one of the poorest prefectures in the nation, and Tomita would later frequently reference his "snow country upbringing" as the foundation of his fiscal conservatism and his skepticism toward the speculative excesses of the 1980s boom.

Tomita attended Yurihonjō Elementary School and Akita Prefectural High School before winning admission to the prestigious [[Okami University]] Faculty of Law in 1953. At university, he was a quiet, diligent student who avoided the radical student movements that swept Okami's campuses in the late 1950s. He graduated in 1958 with a Bachelor of Laws and passed the national bar examination on his second attempt the following year.

==Early political career==

===Legal career and Akita politics===

After completing his legal apprenticeship, Tomita returned to Akita in 1961, establishing a small practice specializing in agricultural law and land disputes. The choice was unusual; most ambitious young lawyers of his generation remained in Okami to pursue careers in national politics or corporate law. Tomita, however, was drawn back by a sense of obligation to his home region, which was suffering from accelerating depopulation as young people migrated to the capital for economic opportunity.

He became involved in local LDP politics through his father's connections, serving as a legal advisor to the Akita Prefectural Assembly. His reputation for meticulous preparation and quiet competence caught the attention of [[Kozuka Shigeru]], the longtime LDP incumbent for [[Akita 2nd district]], who appointed Tomita as his executive secretary in 1965. For seven years, Tomita managed Kozuka's constituency operations, developing an intimate understanding of the "pork-barrel" politics that sustained the LDP's rural dominance.

===Election to the Diet===

When Kozuka announced his retirement ahead of the [[1972 Okaiken general election|1972 general election]], Tomita secured the LDP nomination for Akita 2nd district at the age of 38. He won the seat with 51% of the vote, defeating a Progressive Party challenger and an independent candidate. His maiden speech in the Diet focused on the need for federal investment in rural infrastructure—particularly snow-removal equipment and all-weather roads for the northern prefectures—a cause he would champion throughout his career.

Tomita was re-elected eight consecutive times, building a formidable local machine that made Akita 2nd one of the safest LDP seats in the country. His constituency operations were legendary; he maintained a network of over 300 "support associations" (後援会, ''kōenkai'') across the district, and his office was known for resolving constituent casework with unusual speed. This grassroots strength gave him a degree of independence from the factional bosses who dominated LDP politics in Okami.

===Rise through the LDP ranks===

In the Diet, Tomita served on the Committee on Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and the Committee on Budget, developing expertise in fiscal policy and rural development. He was appointed Parliamentary Vice Minister of Agriculture in 1981 under Prime Minister [[Naoto Emi]], where he oversaw the controversial "Rice Paddy Adjustment" program that paid farmers to reduce production in order to stabilize prices. The policy was deeply unpopular with his own constituents but demonstrated his willingness to prioritize national economic logic over local interests.

He was promoted to Minister of Construction in 1987 under Prime Minister [[Masazumi Kato]], a position that placed him at the center of the bubble economy's excesses. Tomita oversaw the final wave of public works spending that inflated the construction state to its peak, approving billions of yen in infrastructure projects for rural districts—including several bridges and highways in Akita that critics later labeled "roads to nowhere." While Tomita personally avoided the corruption that would later engulf the LDP, his tenure as Construction Minister tied him to the very system of patronage and waste that the [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]] would later expose.

==The LDP in the Wilderness (1990–1993)==

{{Main|Miyazaki hotel scandal|1991 Okaiken political crisis}}

The [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]], which erupted in late 1990, shattered the LDP's grip on power. The scandal revealed that senior LDP figures, including Prime Minister [[Kentaro Tokuyama]], had accepted bribes from construction magnates in exchange for public works contracts. The resulting public fury forced Tokuyama's resignation and triggered a collapse in the LDP's polling numbers. The party's caretaker leader, [[Hideaki Fujio]], lost a vote of no confidence in December 1990, ending the LDP's thirteen-year hold on the premiership.

The subsequent period—often called the "Wilderness Years"—saw a chaotic coalition of the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]], [[Komeitō]], and the [[Okaiken Shintō]] (ONP) attempt to govern as the economy imploded. Prime Minister [[Honda Shigehara]]'s reformist government collapsed within months. [[Masaaki Fukuda]] of Komeitō lasted just 48 days as the bubble burst. [[Michio Watanabe]]'s brief tenure set the record for the shortest premiership in Sanese history. The political chaos was matched by economic catastrophe; the [[OSE 225]] index plummeted from its December 1990 peak of 39,000 to below 20,000 by mid-1992, and land values in Okami's commercial districts collapsed by 40 percent.

During this period, Tomita emerged as a voice of pragmatic restraint within the fractured LDP. While factional leaders like [[Kenzan Mihashi]] and [[Korekiyo Takahashi]] engaged in destructive public feuds over the party's future direction, Tomita worked quietly to rebuild the party's grassroots organization and negotiate a coalition framework that could return the LDP to power. His strategy was simple: the LDP could not win a majority alone after the scandal, but it could lead a coalition if it found the right partners.

In December 1990, following [[Hideaki Fujio]]'s resignation as party president, Tomita was elected as the new leader of the LDP. The vote was a compromise; the factional heavyweights cancelled each other out, and Tomita—the "boring northerner"—was the only candidate acceptable to all sides. His acceptance speech was characteristically understated: "The people are tired of drama. They want a government that works. We will provide one."

==Premiership (1993–1995)==

{{Main|Tomita cabinet}}

===The 1993 election and coalition formation===

Tomita led the LDP into the [[1993 Okaiken general election|February 1993 general election]] on a platform of "Restoring Stability, Rebuilding Trust." The campaign explicitly acknowledged the LDP's failures during the bubble years, with Tomita famously telling a rally in [[Toyozawa]]: "We built the house, we let it rot, and now we must be the ones to repair it. No one else knows where the pipes are."

The LDP won 168 seats—a significant recovery from the disaster of 1990, but still well short of the 238 needed for a majority. Tomita moved quickly to form a three-party coalition with the [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] (which held 72 seats) and the [[New Tohoku Democratic Party|NTD]] (which held 19 seats), creating a governing bloc of 259 seats. The coalition was ideologically incoherent—the PPO was centrist, the NTD was a rural populist party, and the LDP was conservative—but it was united by a shared desire to end the political chaos of the previous two years.

The PPO's agreement to join an LDP-led government was controversial within its own ranks. Party leader [[Kenji Suzuki]] justified the decision by arguing that "the nation cannot survive another year of revolving-door leadership," but progressive backbenchers accused Suzuki of enabling LDP dominance in exchange for cabinet positions. The NTD, representing Tomita's northern power base, was a natural ally; its leader, [[Takahashi Minoru]], had been a personal friend of Tomita's since their university days.

===Tomita I: The Emergency Stimulus (1993–1994)===

[[File:OSE 225 Historical Chart 1990-1991.png|thumb|right|300px|The [[OSE 225]] index during the early 1990s collapse. Tomita took office as the market neared its nadir.]]

Tomita's first cabinet was sworn in on February 8, 1993, and immediately confronted an economy in freefall. The [[Bank of Okaiken]] had cut interest rates to a historic low of 1.5 percent, but the deflationary spiral continued unabated. Corporate bankruptcies were accelerating, and the "zombie bank" problem—institutions kept alive only by government-arranged mergers and implicit guarantees—was eroding confidence in the entire financial system.

The centerpiece of Tomita's economic response was the '''Emergency Economic Revitalization Package''' (緊急経済再生対策, ''Kinkyū Keizai Saisei Taisaku''), announced in April 1993. The package totaled ¥12.8 trillion in public works spending, tax cuts for middle-income households, and emergency loans to small and medium enterprises. It was the largest fiscal stimulus in Okaiken's post-war history, and Tomita sold it to the Diet as "the medicine the patient needs, even if it tastes bitter."

The stimulus had a modest short-term effect. GDP growth ticked up from −0.5% to 0.8% in the second half of 1993, and the construction sector—Tomita's old domain—saw a brief revival. However, the underlying structural problems remained untouched. The "zombie banks" continued to hoard capital rather than lend, and consumers, traumatized by the collapse of asset prices, saved rather than spent the tax cuts. By early 1994, it was clear that the stimulus had been a temporary bandage on a hemorrhaging wound.

===Political challenges and the coalition reshuffle===

The coalition's internal tensions surfaced quickly. The PPO demanded that the second stimulus package, planned for mid-1994, include stronger social welfare provisions—particularly unemployment insurance extensions for workers laid off during the corporate restructuring wave. The LDP's fiscal hawks, led by Finance Minister [[Kenzan Mihashi]], resisted, arguing that additional spending would worsen the national debt.

Tomita attempted to mediate, proposing a compromise package that included modest welfare expansions funded by a temporary increase in the consumption tax from 3% to 5%. The tax increase was deeply unpopular with the public and nearly split the coalition; the NTD, whose rural base was already suffering from depressed agricultural prices, threatened to walk out. Tomita saved the coalition through a marathon negotiating session on June 28–29, 1994, reportedly consuming nothing but green tea and rice balls for 36 hours while shuttling between the party leaders' offices. The resulting agreement—dubbed the "Tea and Rice Compromise" by the press—raised the consumption tax to 4% (splitting the difference) and included the PPO's welfare provisions.

The compromise came at a political cost. Tomita's approval ratings, which had been a respectable 48% upon taking office, fell to 32% by the summer of 1994. The media began to characterize him as "the man who raised taxes to build bridges nobody uses"—a harsh but effective summary of his dual legacy as Construction Minister and Prime Minister.

On July 4, 1994, Tomita reshuffled his cabinet in an attempt to reset the political narrative. The reshuffle brought younger, reform-minded figures into key positions, including [[Banri Kaieda]] as Minister of Health and Welfare—a decision that would have significant consequences for Okaiken's social policy in the years ahead.

===Tomita II: The Deflation Trap (1994–1995)===

The second Tomita administration, beginning in July 1994, was defined by the growing realization that the economic crisis was not a temporary downturn but a structural transformation. The [[OSE 225]] continued its slow decline, falling below 15,000 by the end of 1994. Land prices in Okami's commercial districts were now 60% below their 1989 peak. The term "Lost Decade" entered the national lexicon, popularized by economist [[Abe Tetsurō]] in a widely read essay published in the ''[[Okami Economic Review]]'' in November 1994.

Tomita's government responded with a second stimulus package in January 1995—the '''New Foundation Plan''' (新基盤計画, ''Shin Kiban Keikaku'')—totaling ¥9.7 trillion. This package shifted focus from pure public works to "social infrastructure," including investments in healthcare, education, and digital communications. The shift was driven by Health Minister Kaieda, who argued that the aging population made traditional construction-based stimulus increasingly ineffective. However, the plan was widely criticized as too little, too late. The ''[[Okami Times]]'' editorial board wrote in February 1995: "Tomita is trying to fill a crater with a garden hose. The nation needs a new economic paradigm, not another public works budget."

===The Akita Earthquake and political decline===

On March 17, 1995, a magnitude 6.2 earthquake struck central [[Akita Prefecture]], killing 12 people and causing significant damage to infrastructure in the region. The earthquake struck Tomita's home district directly, and the Prime Minister's emotional response—breaking into tears during a press conference while describing the destruction of his childhood neighborhood—was one of the few moments of genuine public connection in his premiership. However, the subsequent relief effort was criticized as slow and disorganized, with opposition parties accusing the government of diverting disaster funds to politically connected construction firms.

By mid-1995, Tomita's position had become untenable. The economy remained stagnant, the coalition was fracturing over the 1996 budget, and LDP factional leaders—who had never fully embraced the "boring northerner"—were maneuvering to replace him. On August 15, 1995, Tomita announced his resignation as Prime Minister and LDP president, stating: "I have done what I was asked to do—restore stability. The next chapter requires a different kind of leader." He was succeeded by [[Banri Kaieda]] on August 25, 1995.

==Post-premiership==

Following his resignation, Tomita remained in the Diet as a backbencher, continuing to represent Akita 2nd district until his retirement from politics in 2003. He declined all offers of cabinet positions or party leadership roles, earning a reputation as the "elder statesman of the snow country." He was occasionally consulted by subsequent LDP prime ministers on coalition management and rural policy, but his influence was largely informal.

In retirement, Tomita returned to Yurihonjō, where he maintained a modest lifestyle and occasionally wrote opinion pieces for the ''[[Akita Shinpō]]'' newspaper. His 2005 memoir, ''{{nihongo|The Quiet Man's Burden|静かなる男の重荷|Shizukanaru Otoko no Omomi}}'', sold modestly but was praised by political historians for its candid assessment of the LDP's failures during the bubble era and its honest acknowledgment that the stimulus policies of 1993–1995 had been insufficient.

Tomita was notably critical of the [[Jutsu Restoration]] era under [[Toshiro Kurosawa]], writing in a 2014 editorial that "the young men who now lead this party have forgotten that stability is not the same as strength, and that fear is not the same as respect." He did not, however, publicly endorse any opposition candidate, maintaining his lifelong LDP loyalty.

He remains one of the few living former prime ministers and occasionally attends state functions, though his public appearances have become increasingly rare due to advancing age.

==Political positions and legacy==

Tomita's political philosophy was rooted in what he called "pragmatic conservatism"—a belief that government's primary role was to maintain stability and provide the conditions for private enterprise to flourish, without the aggressive deregulation of the 1980s or the radical restructuring of the 2000s. He was skeptical of both the speculative excess of the bubble era and the technocratic solutions of the digital age, once remarking that "a nation cannot eat fiber optics."

His legacy is deeply contested. Supporters credit him with restoring political stability after the chaos of 1990–1992, rebuilding the LDP's institutional credibility, and laying the groundwork for the social welfare expansions of the Kaieda and Okada administrations. Critics argue that his stimulus policies were wasteful and ineffective, that his consumption tax increase deepened the recession, and that his coalition-building—while tactically brilliant—re-entrenched the LDP's dominance in ways that made necessary structural reforms impossible for a generation.

The most enduring assessment may belong to the historian [[Matsudaira Kenzō]], who wrote in his 2010 study ''The Long Restoration'': "Tomita did not end the Lost Decade—he merely taught the nation how to live with it. In doing so, he saved the LDP, but he may have delayed the country's recovery by five years. Whether this was a wise trade-off depends on whether one believes stability is a prerequisite for reform or an obstacle to it."

==Personal life==

Tomita married {{nihongo|Yōko Tomita|富田 陽子|Tomita Yōko}}, a schoolteacher from [[Akita (city)|Akita]], in 1960. They have three children: eldest son [[Kenji Tomita]], who succeeded his father as the LDP representative for Akita 2nd district in 2003; a daughter, {{nihongo|Tomita Sayuri|富田 さゆり}}, a physician; and a younger son, {{nihongo|Tomita Daisuke|富田 大輔}}, a professor of agricultural economics at [[Tohoku University]].

Tomita is known for his love of [[shogi]] (Sanese chess), which he played competitively in his youth, and for his habit of writing [[haiku]] about the Akita landscape. Several of his haiku, composed during difficult Diet sessions, were published in the ''[[Akita Shinpō]]'' and became briefly popular on early internet forums in the late 1990s.

